// =============================================================================
// historical-research-course — "Historical Research, Level 1: The 1619
// Project as the Case"
// =============================================================================
// Darrell, 2026-09-23: "Let's use the 1619 project as a history or historical
// research 1 level competencies based on those professors work..." then:
// "Word first research and then Lessons as our Ways and documentation
// mandated..." This is the History department's second course. The first
// (history-course.js) set the standard before the story; this one teaches the
// CRAFT — the eight Level-1 competencies of historical research — on one
// living case: the 1619 Project (The New York Times Magazine, August 2019),
// the professors who answered it in writing, the fact-checker who spoke, and
// the correction the record made of itself.
//
// WORD FIRST (DR-0127 / DR-0282). Every competency is a command before it is
// a skill. The Word tells the researcher to prove all things, to go to the
// eyewitness, to establish every word at the mouth of two or three witnesses,
// to hear the matter before answering it, to weigh with a just weight and no
// respect of persons, to regard reproof, to know what is settled and what
// must be searched out, and to write it in order so the reader may know the
// certainty of the words of truth. Each verse was pulled verbatim from the
// repo's own KJV (app/public/bible/kjv) before a line of lesson was written
// and is re-pinned by historical-research-course.test.js.
//
// THE ARC — eight competencies, one case:
//   1. Prove all things — a claim is a claim until the record answers it
//      (1 Thessalonians 5:21; Acts 17:11; Proverbs 14:15)
//   2. Go to the record — the eyewitness before the retelling
//      (Luke 1:1-4; Job 8:8-10; Deuteronomy 32:7)
//   3. Two or three witnesses — corroborate before you repeat
//      (Deuteronomy 19:15; 2 Corinthians 13:1; Proverbs 18:17; Exodus 23:1-2)
//   4. A fact and an interpretation are two different things
//      (Proverbs 18:13; John 7:24; 2 Timothy 2:15; Proverbs 19:2)
//   5. First in his own cause — read a dispute by its evidence, not its side
//      (Proverbs 18:17; Proverbs 11:1; Leviticus 19:15; James 2:9)
//   6. The correction — a record that changes toward the truth
//      (Proverbs 28:13; Proverbs 13:18; Proverbs 12:19; Psalms 15:4)
//   7. What the Word settles and what the record must supply
//      (Exodus 21:16; Deuteronomy 24:7; Acts 17:26; 1 Timothy 1:10; Matthew 25:40)
//   8. Write it in order — the sourced paragraph
//      (Luke 1:3-4; Habakkuk 2:2; Proverbs 22:20-21; 1 Peter 3:15)
//
// TEACH THE WORD, DO NOT DEBATE IT (DR-0098) and SPEAK ESTABLISHED FACT
// (DR-0100). The moral verdict on slavery is not a research finding: the Word
// settled it (man-stealing is capital, all nations are of one blood), and this
// course never stages it as open. What research supplies is what happened,
// when, to whom, on what record — and there the course states documented
// fact plainly with its date: the White Lion at Point Comfort in August 1619;
// the earlier arrivals of 1526 and 1565; Somerset in 1772; Dunmore's
// proclamation in 1775; the Declaration in 1776; the magazine in August 2019;
// the five historians' letter and the editor's reply in December 2019; the
// Atlantic essay in January 2020; the fact-checker's account and the editor's
// note in March 2020; the Pulitzer in May 2020; the book in November 2021.
// Where a question is genuinely open — how far the protection of slavery
// moved particular colonists toward independence — the course says so
// narrowly and teaches how to weigh it by evidence, never "you decide."
//
// THEIR WORDS, FETCHED NOT REMEMBERED (DR-0580). Every voice below was probed
// on a GitHub runner against its named source before it was written in
// (history-voices-witness run 35928051751, 2026-09-23): the pages that
// answered are the only pages quoted. Sources that did not answer (a
// Wikisource page for Somerset, for John Rolfe's letter, for Jefferson's
// Notes) are named in the timeline as the record to check, never quoted.
//
// AGE BANDS FROM THE FIRST COMMIT (DR-0497 / DR-0498): levels.teen and
// levels.senior on every lesson, measured on the ladder, the fullness floor
// and the overlap ceiling.
//
// TEACHING, NOT A SUBSTITUTE FOR THE RECORD — carried on the meta. Every
// document this course names can be read in full at the record it points to,
// and the student is meant to read it.
// =============================================================================

