      teen: 'Here is the first skill. Before you agree or disagree, prove it. The Word says it plainly. "Prove all things; hold fast that which is good" (1 Thessalonians 5:21). That is a command. It is not a mood.\n\nIn August 2019 a big magazine printed a set of essays. It was called the 1619 Project. It said a lot of things. Some people loved it. Some people were angry. Most of them had not done the first step. The first step is to sort the claims.\n\nThere are three kinds of claims. A fact claim says something happened on a date. A ship landed in Virginia in 1619. That is a fact claim. A record can settle it. A frame claim says this is the best way to tell the story. Start the story in 1619 instead of 1776. That is a frame claim. You test it by asking what it shows and what it hides. A cause claim says one thing happened because of another. The colonists broke away to protect slavery. That is a cause claim. It is the hardest kind. You need what people said at the time.\n\nThe Bereans did this. Paul preached to them. They did not just clap. They "searched the scriptures daily, whether those things were so" (Acts 17:11). The Word calls them noble for it. It also warns the other way. "The simple believeth every word: but the prudent man looketh well to his going" (Proverbs 14:15).\n\nOne more thing before the task. The 1619 Project was not one person. It was a team. Nikole Hannah-Jones led it. Writers, poets, photographers and editors worked on it for a long time. They used real records. That is skill. You can honor the skill and still sort the claims. A good researcher does both at once. Honoring the work is not the same as agreeing with every sentence. Testing a sentence is not the same as attacking the work.\n\nSo here is your job. Write down three claims you have heard about the 1619 Project. Put a letter by each one. F for fact. W for way of telling. C for cause. Do not say if they are true yet. Just sort them. That is the first skill. Everything else in this course builds on it.',
