    lesson: 'Historical research begins with a command, and the command is older than the discipline: "Prove all things; hold fast that which is good" (1 Thessalonians 5:21). It is addressed to ordinary believers, which means the method belongs to a family at a kitchen table as much as to a university. A claim about the past is neither true nor false because of who made it, how many people repeat it, or how confidently it is put. It is a claim until the record answers it, and the first competency is to know what kind of claim you are holding, because different kinds are answered by different records.\n\nIn August 2019 The New York Times Magazine published the 1619 Project, a set of essays led by the journalist Nikole Hannah-Jones that took the landing of enslaved Africans in Virginia in 1619 as the point from which to retell the American story, with slavery’s consequences and the contributions of Black Americans at the center. It was the work of a large team — journalists, essayists, poets, photographers and editors — built on documents and on the historians it consulted, and it carried documented history to an audience no scholarly book reaches; that is skill in the service of education, and a researcher says so plainly. Within a year it had been praised, attacked, corrected in one sentence, and awarded a prize, and almost everyone who had an opinion about it had skipped the first step. The first step is to sort its claims.\n\nThere are three kinds. A fact claim says that something happened at a time and place: a ship called the White Lion put enslaved Africans ashore at Point Comfort in 1619. A document can settle that, and one does. A frame claim says that this is the truest way to tell the story: that the nation’s life is better understood from 1619 than from 1776. No single document settles a frame; you test it by asking what it brings into view and what it leaves out, and a frame can be illuminating and still not be the whole. A cause claim says that one thing happened because of another: that the colonists declared independence in part to protect slavery. That is the hardest kind, because it is a claim about motive, and motive is established only from what the actors said and did at the time, weighed against everything else they said and did.\n\nThe Word gives the posture for all three. The Bereans "received the word with all readiness of mind, and searched the scriptures daily, whether those things were so" (Acts 17:11), and they are called noble for it. Notice both halves: they were ready to hear, and they went to the record. The opposite posture is named too: "The simple believeth every word: but the prudent man looketh well to his going" (Proverbs 14:15). Swallowing a claim whole and spitting it out whole are the same failure wearing different clothes; neither one has proved anything. And underneath the sorting stands the measure that does not move: "Thy word is true from the beginning" (Psalms 119:160). The researcher’s claims are provisional; His standard is not.\n\nSo the exercise this week is deliberately modest. Take three claims you have heard about the 1619 Project, from any direction, and write beside each which kind it is. Do not decide whether any of them is true. A student who can hold an unsorted claim without either believing it or dismissing it has learned the first competency, and the seven that follow depend on it.',
